    source: "text",
    title: "Beacon retro, three weeks on",
    tags: ["work", "on-call"],
    raw: "wrote up the beacon duplicate-events retro for myself before the team one. 3 weeks since the fix went in (2.13.0) and the duplicate ratio has been flat zero. 41,212 dupe rows removed, 1,912 parcels had been double counted in ledgerline for that billing week.\n\nwhat went wrong: the consumer commits to postgres before it commits the kafka offset, so a crash in between redelivers the batch, and parcel_events had no unique key, so the second insert just worked. i spent the first two days convinced the carrier API was replaying events, because that happened in the spring and callum said it was probably that again. i never tried to reproduce on our side until the third day. the chaos script killing the go consumer mid-batch took 20 min to write and showed it immediately. also priya asked during the retry queue walkthrough why parcel_events has no unique key, and i waved it off. she was right. (the lesson sounds neat but really it was just a mistake in order of operations)\n\nwhat went well: desmond's plan for the index, yuki's runbook for pausing the ledgerline export, and the dry-run flag on the backfill script, which saved me from sending finance a wrong file.\n\nstill open: (1) consumer-lag alert at 5000 is noisy, threshold change not made yet (2) chaos test is not in CI (3) postmortem summary for the eng channel, mine to write, i keep not doing it. renata has asked twice.",
    cleaned: "I wrote up the Beacon duplicate-events retro for myself before the team one. It has been three weeks since the fix went in (2.13.0) and the duplicate ratio has been flat at zero. 41,212 duplicate rows were removed, and 1,912 parcels had been double-counted in Ledgerline for that billing week.\n\nWhat went wrong: the consumer commits to PostgreSQL before it commits the Kafka offset, so a crash in between redelivers the batch, and parcel_events had no unique key, so the second insert simply worked. I spent the first two days convinced the carrier API was replaying events, because that happened in the spring and Callum said it was probably that again. I did not try to reproduce on our side until the third day. The chaos script that killed the Go consumer mid-batch took 20 minutes to write and showed the problem immediately.\n\nPriya also asked during the retry-queue walkthrough why parcel_events has no unique key, and I waved it off. She was right. The lesson sounds neat, but really it was just a mistake in order of operations.\n\nWhat went well: Desmond's plan for the index, Yuki's runbook for pausing the Ledgerline export, and the dry-run flag on the backfill script, which saved me from sending finance a wrong file.\n\nStill open: (1) the consumer-lag alert at 5,000 messages is noisy and the threshold change has not been made; (2) the chaos test is not in CI; (3) the postmortem summary for the engineering channel is mine to write and I keep not doing it. Renata has asked twice.",
